IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`
IMPORT `ccc30-forms-actors`
IMPORT `ccc30-forms-appearance`

-- ccc30-tests-forms-appearance.l4 -- tests for ccc30-forms-appearance.l4 (Forms 6, 6.2, 6.3, 9, 10, 10.1, 11, 11.1 and 12, raw lines 17947-18001 and 18074-18326).
-- Every expected value is written by hand from the words of the Form named in the comment beside it, never from the module's output.
-- JUST TRUE means "the Form says so"; JUST FALSE is the text's own no; NOTHING is "not covered", never "no". A #TRACE prints its outcome and no assertion line,
-- so each trace has its expected outcome written above it (read by eye), and the same duty is not asserted twice.

§ `Tests -- Form 6, the summons`

-- "(Ignore, if not filled in)": not filled in
#ASSERT `form 6 paragraph (a) is to be ignored` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- filled in: the instruction does not apply
#ASSERT `form 6 paragraph (a) is to be ignored` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- failed to appear or attend, no lawful excuse
#ASSERT `form 6 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- "unless you have a lawful excuse"
#ASSERT `form 6 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- did not fail: outside the sentence's words
#ASSERT `form 6 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- did not fail: outside the sentence's words, excuse or not
#ASSERT `form 6 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act or to attend court, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- committed the offence: a warrant for arrest may be issued
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` TRUE `forms 6 to 11.1 a warrant for the arrest of the person may be issued` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- ... and the person may be liable to a fine
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` TRUE `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to a fine` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- ... or to imprisonment
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` TRUE `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to imprisonment` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- ... or to both
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` TRUE `forms 6 to 11.1 the person may be liable to both a fine and imprisonment` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- a consequence the sentence does not name: not covered
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` TRUE `forms 6 to 11.1 another consequence` EQUALS NOTHING
-- no offence committed: the "If" is unmet
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` FALSE `forms 6 to 11.1 a warrant for the arrest of the person may be issued` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- no offence committed: JUST FALSE whatever the consequence asked
#ASSERT `form 6 if the person commits an offence under subsection 145(3), the Form states that` FALSE `forms 6 to 11.1 another consequence` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- does not comply
#ASSERT `form 6 the summons may be cancelled` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- is charged with committing an offence after
#ASSERT `form 6 the summons may be cancelled` FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- both limbs
#ASSERT `form 6 the summons may be cancelled` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- neither limb: the "If" is unmet
-- RECONCILED 2026-11-12: was `#ASSERT `form 6 the summons may be cancelled` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE`; the name is a "may" that gives a power or liberty and its "If" gate is unmet; the text limits the grant in its own wording and is silent outside the limit, so the answer is NOTHING, not a "may not" (name-test sweep, fork F-W; the other reading, a failed "If" as the text's own no, is JUST FALSE).
#ASSERT `form 6 the summons may be cancelled` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- "as a result, you may be detained in custody", once cancelled
#ASSERT `form 6 the person may be detained in custody as a result of the cancellation` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- not cancelled: the result clause does not apply
#ASSERT `form 6 the person may be detained in custody as a result of the cancellation` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Form 6 (b): 14 days until the day set out. Expected from the text: attending at day 10 -> FULFILLED; attending at day 14 (the last day) -> FULFILLED; never attending by day 15 -> BREACH.
#TRACE `form 6 the accused must attend court` 14 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the accused` DOES `form 6 attends court as ordered` AT 10

#TRACE `form 6 the accused must attend court` 14 AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the accused` DOES `form 6 attends court as ordered` AT 14

#TRACE `form 6 the accused must attend court` 14 AT 0 WITH
    WAITUNTIL 15

§ `Tests -- Form 6.2, summons to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act`

-- failed to appear or attend, no lawful excuse
#ASSERT `form 6.2 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- "unless you have a lawful excuse"
#ASSERT `form 6.2 warning: unless the person has a lawful excuse, failing to appear for the purposes of the Identification of Criminals Act, as required in the summons is an offence under subsection 145(3)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
